  "dom_id": "step-q127721",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the limit  $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-{t}^{2}))i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t-1})j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\sqrt{t-1})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-{t}^{2}) & =1-{0}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{0-1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\sqrt{1-t} & =\\sqrt{1-0}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=i-j+k.$",
